# Evolution keeps its own libraries in a private directory of its own
# (privlibdir, /usr/lib/evolution) rather than on the loader's default
# search path, so a module that dlopens libevolution-mail.so/
# libevolution-shell.so has to record that directory in its own RUNPATH or
# it cannot load at all outside a process that already has them resident —
# every module Evolution installs under /usr/lib/evolution/modules/*.so
# already carries exactly this RUNPATH, this one included. lintian's
# custom-library-search-path tag still fires on it, because its exemption
# for a package's own private libdir only matches /usr/lib/<source-package>,
# which this is not; since the RUNPATH itself is required for the module to
# load, the fix is this override arguing the entry is deliberate, not
# removing the RUNPATH.
evolution-jmap: custom-library-search-path RUNPATH /usr/lib/evolution [usr/lib/evolution/modules/module-jmap-configuration.so]

# Same justification as above, for the three modules item 80 stage 1 gave
# the identical RUNPATH to (they dlopen libevolution-mail.so transitively
# through jmap-config's own need for evo-sys). Their install directories
# come from pkg-config (libedata-book-1.2/libedata-cal-2.0/libebackend-1.2's
# own backenddir/moduledir) rather than this package's own layout, so the
# path up to the backend/module directory name itself can carry a multiarch
# triplet on some distros and not on others; the leading `*` absorbs that
# instead of hardcoding one distro's layout.
evolution-jmap: custom-library-search-path RUNPATH /usr/lib/evolution [usr/lib/*evolution-data-server/addressbook-backends/libebookbackendjmap.so]
evolution-jmap: custom-library-search-path RUNPATH /usr/lib/evolution [usr/lib/*evolution-data-server/calendar-backends/libecalbackendjmap.so]
evolution-jmap: custom-library-search-path RUNPATH /usr/lib/evolution [usr/lib/*evolution-data-server/registry-modules/module-jmap-backend.so]
